WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.340
People have been harnessing the power of wind
for thousands of years, the earliest recorded

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.829
evidence of this can be seen over 1000 years
ago in Persia. But these machine have evolved

00:00:08.829 --> 00:00:15.239
from simple devices used to crush grain and
pump water to towering monsters generating enough electricity to power entire towns.

00:00:15.239 --> 00:00:18.910
Traditional wind turbines come in many shapes

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.910
and designs, but they have all given way to a
fairly consistent 3 blade design. Today we

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.460
are going to answer the question. Why do wind
turbines have 3 blades?

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.210
We can imagine each blade as a wrench tightening
a nut. If we increase the length of the wrench

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.920
we can generate more torque. That’s the
force that causes rotation. Likewise if we

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:41.840
add a second wrench we can apply even more
force. The same principle applies to wind

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.969
turbines. So naturally you may think, why
not add as many blades and make them as long

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:48.840
as possible.
The biggest wind turbine has a diameter of

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.420
164 metres. Each blade weighs 33 tonnes and
each cost hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.590
to manufacture.
So more blades equals more weight and more

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.320
cost to manufacture. So maybe less blades
is better?

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.370
Let’s compare our standard 3 blade design
to its immediate competitors a 2 blade turbine

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.140
and a 4 blade turbine. Let’s assume they
have the same blade design for now.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.590
We can easily eliminate the 4 blade design
with a quick cost analysis. Each of these

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.530
blades costs a lot of money. Adding a fourth
blade provides such a marginal increase in

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.930
performance that it does not justify the additional
cost,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.260
So its down to 2 and 3 blades.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.810
A 2 bladed design can match the performance
of a 3 bladed design by increasing

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:33.070
the chord of the blade by 50%, which eliminates
the cost advantage so that’s pointless.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:38.890
Or we can increase its rotational speed by
22.5%. Turbines with 2 blades will spin faster

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.890
in the same wind, due to the reduced drag
they experience, but spinning faster

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.140
is a negative. Let’s explore why. 
A faster spinning blade will generate more

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:56.950
noise. This is what a wind turbine sounds
like up-close. Yeah people don’t like living

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:02.460
next to these things, so we need to minimise
the noise they make, by minimising their speed.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.570
Next we need to worry about centrifugal forces.
As the blades spin faster, their apparent

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:10.890
weight increases. Thus the central hub and
the blades need to be stronger to resist the

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:16.079
additional stress, again this adds cost. This
is what can happen when a turbines breaks

00:02:16.079 --> 00:02:26.890
fail in strong winds.
So a 3 blade design can generate more power

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:31.780
at slower rotational speeds than a two blade
design, while being more cost efficient than

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:37.390
a four bladed option. So the 3 bladed design
is our goldilocks choice. Not too much, not

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:47.760
too little.

